From 1d66a8811af97c15c180e47b03607b20a7173447 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:40:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/19] wifi: rtl8192f-soc: set TX power and recalibrate on channel
 switch

The BB init table leaves the per-rate TxAGC group (0xe00..0xe1c and the
CCK fields at 0x838 and 0x86c) at zero because the vendor rewrites it at
run time from the board's factory calibration: a driver that never
touches it drives every frame from the bottom of the PA ladder, which is
where the codeword 0 sits.  Program the default level the vendor itself
falls back to when a board carries no factory array - 0x28 for the OFDM
and HT groups and 0x24 for the CCK fields, clamped by POWER_RANGE_CHECK
to 0..0x3f.  A calibrated board does not use those constants: the
factory record, taken from the flash MIB partition as one nvmem cell and
indexed by the offsets of the vendor record layout, carries one TXAGC
codeword per 2.4 GHz channel per rate group plus the two-path difference
nibbles, and a zero entry means the board stores no level for that
channel, which is also how the vendor tests it.

A channel change is where the vendor recomputes both that level and the IQ
calibration (SwChnl() calls PHY_IQCalibrate() at 8192cd_hw.c 7523 and
SetTxPowerLevel() at :7461), because the quadrature error is measured at
the transmitter frequency; run both on every switch rather than only at
bring-up.

Digital pre-distortion (DPK) was tried here and removed again: the vendor
sequence loads a coefficient block and switches predistortion on around
every IQ calibration, but its coefficients are meant to be retrained per
unit, and enabling them from the shipped defaults silences the
transmitter: the beacon counter stays at zero while the same build
without DPK sends at the nominal rate.  The stock firmware likewise
leaves the whole 0xb00 block at zero.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
---
 .../net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/calib.c |   6 +-
 .../net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/core.c  |  14 +-
 .../net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/hw.c    | 235 +++++++++++++++++-
 .../realtek/rtl8192f-soc/rtl8192f_soc.h       |  29 +++
 4 files changed, 272 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/calib.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/calib.c
@@ -8,8 +8,12 @@
  * Simplifications vs the vendor flow (all noted inline):
  * - single IQK pass, no 3-round simularity comparison / final candidate
  * - path A only (RTL8192F-SoC is 1T1R)
- * - no DPK off/on around IQK, no DPK tracking
  * - no thermal-meter triggered re-calibration (init-time one-shot IQK)
+ * - no DPK: the vendor's predistortion runs on its own per-unit
+ *   calibration data, and enabling it from the default coefficients
+ *   silences the transmitter (measured: no beacon reaches the air with
+ *   it on).  The shipped firmware also leaves the whole 0xb00 block at
+ *   zero
  * - external PA/LNA and cut-version A branches dropped (SoC internal PA)
  * - no continuous-TX / MP-mode guards
  */
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/core.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtl8192f-soc/core.c
@@ -805,10 +805,14 @@ static int rtl8192f_soc_probe(struct pla
 		goto err_free_hw;
 	}
 
+	ret = rtl8192f_read_calib(priv);
+	if (ret == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+		goto err_free_hw;
+
 	/* Address precedence: a DT property (mac-address / local-mac-address,
 	 * which a bootloader or packaging layer can fill in), then an nvmem
-	 * cell ("mac-address" from the factory MIB partition in flash, which
